@@ -0,0 +1,168 @@
+# Crem API sketch for Hydra
+
+This branch starts at crem `v0.1.1.0`
+(`f06f8b47ac83d3e7448493bdee374c5f952f7f4c`), the version used by Hydra's
+HeadLogic spike. It prototypes three library changes. Hydra tests this checkout
+as a local Cabal package throughout its eight workflows and six state owners.
+Five rules remove reply-handling phases, workflow execution needs no rendering
+dictionaries, and owner compositions share one definition for execution and
+diagrams. The protocol rules, Program instructions and persistence formats stay
+the same.
+
+## An effect can choose a typed destination
+
+[`BaseMachine.hs`](../src/Crem/BaseMachine.hs) adds `Transition` and
+`ChooseTransition`. In the existing constructor, the destination type is chosen
+outside the effect:
+
+```haskell
+ActionResult :: AllowedTransition topology from to
+             => m (output, state to)
+             -> ActionResult m topology state from output
+```
+
+The new constructor puts that choice inside the effect:
+
+```haskell
+Transition :: AllowedTransition topology from to
+           => output -> state to -> Transition topology state from output
+
+ChooseTransition :: m (Transition topology state from output)
+                 -> ActionResult m topology state from output
+```
+
+[`Transaction.hs`](../examples/Crem/Example/Transaction.hs) uses it:
+
+```haskell
+step Check transaction = ChooseTransition $ do
+  reply <- applyTransaction transaction
+  case reply of
+    Right after -> do
+      decide "ledger accepts transaction" True
+      pure $ Transition "accepted" (Applied after)
+    Left reason -> do
+      decide "ledger accepts transaction" False
+      pure $ Transition "wait" (Awaiting reason)
+```
+
+Both edges must be in the topology. There is no separate phase whose only job is
+to inspect `reply`. The effect here is a free `Program`; its
+[interpreter](../examples/Crem/Example/Transaction/Program.hs) supplies a ledger
+reply and records queries and named decisions. It has no rule for choosing a
+Crem phase. Running the program still gives a decision trace.
+
+This is a small API example, not Hydra's ReqTx algorithm: integers stand for the
+transaction and resulting ledger value, and waiting is terminal. It does not
+implement retry eligibility, staging, confirmation, or persistence.
+
+`runActionResult` handles both constructors. Existing `ActionResult` construction
+remains valid; code pattern-matching on it must handle `ChooseTransition` too.
+An eventual major release could use a single constructor around `m (Transition
+...)`; retaining both here makes the example changes easier to compare.
+
+## Execution does not require rendering dictionaries
+
+[`StateMachine.hs`](../src/Crem/StateMachine.hs) adds `Executable`, a leaf with no
+`SingI`, `SingKind`, `Eq`, `Show`, or `RenderableVertices` requirements. It uses the
+same base machine runner and allowed-edge constraints as `Basic`. The test's
+`Phase` type has none of those instances.
+
+`Basic` remains available when the caller wants to retain rendering dictionaries.
+`withoutRendering` turns every leaf of an existing composition into `Executable`,
+preserving actions, state and inspection callbacks. Hydra defines its owner
+compositions once with `Basic` for diagrams, then calls `withoutRendering` when
+starting their live runtimes. The transformation does not execute actions and
+is safe to repeat.
+
+[`Render.hs`](../src/Crem/Render/Render.hs) now returns `Either String UntypedGraph`
+from `machineAsGraph`. An `Executable` leaf makes rendering fail explicitly,
+including when it is nested in a composition. `renderFlow` reports the missing
+metadata too. To adapt an existing caller:
+
+```haskell
+renderUntypedGraph <$> machineAsGraph machine
+```
+
+This is an additive execution constructor, not a full redesign of rendering.
+The existing stateless helpers and `unrestrictedMachine` retain their rendering
+behavior. A broader split between executable and renderable machines needs a
+separate compatibility decision.
+
+## Inspection follows retained state
+
+[`StateInspection.hs`](../src/Crem/StateInspection.hs) defines `StateInspector`:
+one callback describes the state, another returns a `StateCheck`. Both work
+at every phase. `InspectableState inspector state` installs the hook; the old
+`InitialState state` leaves it absent. `withInitialState` preserves the hook when
+execution or decider replay replaces the state. Existing base combinators already
+preserve `InitialState`, so they need no observer-specific implementations.
+`withStateInspector` attaches or replaces the hook after a persistence adapter
+has restored an existential initial phase, without repeating that adapter's
+pattern match.
+
+[`Inspect.hs`](../src/Crem/Inspect.hs) walks the composition tree:
+
+- `inspectStates` returns descriptions, with `Nothing` for uninspected leaves.
+- `checkStates` returns `NotInspected`, `CheckPassed`, or `FoundThunk` for each
+  leaf, including an inactive alternative. Paths identify the leaf's position.
+
+Descriptions may redact data. Checking does not evaluate descriptions, run
+actions, or inspect action closures and effect environments. These are trusted
+callbacks, not a serialization format or a proof of arbitrary closure strictness.
+
+`describeOnly` installs a description and reports `NotInspected` for checks.
+Hydra uses it for workflow and owner phase labels: those states can contain opaque
+machines and lazy ledger projections, so the labels do not imply a complete
+strictness check. Where a state
+has a `NoThunks` instance, `checkNoThunks` adapts it to the checking callback.
+A checker may also return `NotInspected` for particular phases.
+
+The old unconditional `NoThunks (StateMachineT ...)` instance is removed: it
+accepted `Basic _` without examining its state. A caller must now choose how to
+handle `NotInspected`; an unknown result must not silently count as a pass.
+`InitialState` pattern matches must also handle `InspectableState`.
+
+## Run and review
+
+With GHC 9.6.7 and Cabal:
+
+```sh
+cabal build all -ferrors
+cabal repl crem:test:crem-spec -f-errors
+# At the GHCi prompt:
+:main
+:quit
+cabal test crem-spec -ferrors --test-show-details=direct
+cabal run crem-doctests -ferrors -- --no-nix
+bash scripts/check-transition-types.sh
+cabal run transaction-sketch
+```
+
+The demo prints the topology, then the accepting and waiting traces and state
+checks. Diagram generation is in the executable, separate from the rule and
+the Program interpreter.
+
+The first state checks report `FoundThunk` on GHC 9.6.7: execution retains a
+lazy phase value, even though its payload fields are strict. This is an
+observation about evaluation, not a protocol failure. The demo checks before
+printing the state description, which could otherwise force that value and hide
+the result. The inspector does not force state to make a check pass.
+
+The doctest `--no-nix` option makes it use Cabal's package environment when run
+from Hydra's Nix shell. The default Nix mode misses dependencies installed by
+Cabal in this standalone checkout.
+
+[`ApiSketchSpec.hs`](../spec/Crem/ApiSketchSpec.hs) checks both replies, trace order,
+effects running once, hoisting and sequencing, missing rendering metadata,
+inspection after execution and composition, decider replay, and a deliberately
+suspended payload. The [typecheck script](../scripts/check-transition-types.sh)
+first compiles the allowed counterpart, then requires the compiler to reject an
+`Accepted -> Checking` edge selected inside an effect. This checks topology
+enforcement without treating an unrelated compiler failure as success.
+
+These changes do not strengthen the protocol evidence carried by a state.
+Self-transitions remain legal, same-phase data can still be replaced, and a
+machine author can declare an overly permissive topology. Hydra still needs its
+owner commands and candidate checks. No throughput or allocation improvement is
+claimed; the additional constructors and observer closures need measurement in
+the actual node before wider adoption.
